{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HQtziGu39ocvGamacdo1YZ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t## 2026-09-01 — WI-572: the approval act is the adjudicator's, on trunk\n2\t\n3\t**Spec of record:** `../plans/2026-09-01-approval-act-adjudicator-only.md`\n4\t(the owner's ruling, 2026-09-01, recorded in\n5\t`../log.md` from `2026-09-01-owner-ruling-approval-act.md`). Serialized behind\n6\tWI-571 (the copy-scope row); both touch `intake.py` / `baseline_snapshot.py`.\n7\t\n8\t**In one line:** a worker lane may author `Drafted` spine rows and amend cell\n9\ttext, but the approval act — the `Status` flip into `Approved`/`Founded` and\n10\tthe `docs/archive/last_approved/` copy that anchors it — is the adjudicator's,\n11\tperformed on the serial trunk side.\n12\t\n13\t### The baseline this row is measured against\n14\t\n15\tEvery commit that moved an `\"Approved\"` string in a spine registry before the\n16\truling, classified by where it happened:\n17\t\n18\t- **1 worker-lane flip** — `580df781` (WI-508 slice 6), whose next review round\n19\t  returned CHANGES-REQUESTED against exactly those flips.\n20\t- **4 lanes minted rows born `Approved`**, skipping the brief entirely —\n21\t  `8848f6fb` (WI-483), `ad2222df` (WI-500), `69e4a854` (WI-501), `0cfb2e6f`\n22\t  (WI-507).\n23\t- The rest were trunk sittings or the pre-ladder rename.\n24\t\n25\tfig: 17 commits, classified by subject; `git log --format='%h|%s'\n26\t-S'\"Approved\"' fd86e47f -- docs/requirements/system-requirements.toml\n27\tdocs/requirements/low-level-requirements.toml docs/test/test-cases.toml\n28\tdocs/requirements/system-requirements.csv\n29\tdocs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv docs/test/test-cases.csv`\n30\tat `fd86e47f`.\n31\t\n32\t### Deliverables\n33\t\n34\t**1. A lane's merge is refused when its delta performs an approval act.**\n35\t`acceptance_record.staged_approval_acts` reports a `Status` crossing INTO\n36\t`Approved`/`Founded` and a row that arrives already claiming one;\n37\t`lane_approval_refusal` words the refusal, naming each row, its registry, the\n38\tshape of act, and every `docs/archive/last_approved/` file the branch wrote.\n39\t`integrate._approval_act_refusal` is the rung, beside `_minted_id_refusal`\n40\twhose shape it copies exactly — the merge base, and the ladder placement.\n41\t\n42\tConstruction-first, as the plan required: `staged_spine_amendments` already\n43\tdiffs the merged commit and EXEMPTS a row whose Status moved; the new reader\n44\treports precisely that exempted set. All three readers (plus\n45\t`staged_drafted_rows`) now share ONE two-tree walk, `_spine_row_sides`,\n46\textracted rather than copied. Verified against the record itself: the reader\n47\treproduces the census above exactly — four flips at `580df781`, and the\n48\tborn-`Approved` rows of all four lanes.\n49\t\n50\tThe judgement lives in `acceptance_record`, not in the merge slot, on LLR-178's\n51\tseparation: the coordinator that merges is not the reader that decides what a\n52\tspine delta did. That also kept `integrate.py`'s size bump to a new rung's\n53\tirreducible core.\n54\t\n55\t**2. The first-approval adjudication arm.** If a lane may not approve what it\n56\tauthored, something must. `intake._first_approval_drafts` (trigger a2) mints ONE\n57\t`brief = \"first-approval\"` adjudication per merge over the `Drafted` rows the\n58\tdelta added or amended — the exact mirror of trigger (a), one section below it.\n59\t`agent_common.SPINE_APPROVAL_RUNGS` names the DevStg rung each spine tier is\n60\tapproved into so the dial can answer whether that tier is loop-held; a rung the\n61\tdial HOLDS is not minted (the owner approves those, through the approval brief,\n62\tas today), and an unmapped tier is held. (That table was `intake.APPROVAL_RUNG`\n63\tuntil REVIEW-A; see \"The dial filter the brief did not have\" below.)\n64\t\n65\t`prompts/adjudicate-first-approval.template.md` is the fifth adjudicator brief,\n66\twith `adjudicate_brief.first_approval_values` behind it and its own verdict\n67\tgrammar (`OUTCOME: APPROVE|RETURN rows=N`). The brief renders each row's WHOLE\n68\tCHAIN, which is the owner's stated reason the act is the adjudicator's — so\n69\t`trace.reattest_model`'s `chain_of` closure became the public\n70\t`trace.spine_chain` + `chain_buckets`. The model's own `rows` list carries only\n71\twhat changed or is `Drafted`: the right answer to the re-attest brief's question\n72\tand exactly the wrong one here, where the settled parent and the passing sibling\n73\ttest ARE the evidence.\n74\t\n75\t`integrate._adjudication_lane` exempts the actor the ruling names. The\n76\tconcurrency half the owner asked for was **already built** and is not\n77\tre-implemented: an adjudication row is not `ordinary`, so\n78\t`dispatch._branch_exclusive` already runs its lane alone. The ruling is what\n79\tpoints the act at that guarantee.\n80\t\n81\t**3. The amendment arm's aftermath is stated and true.** The template's closing\n82\t\"the flip, if one is owed, is the mechanical tool's act, not yours\" went false\n83\twhen OI-45 (b) retired that tool, so a MEANING verdict on a loop-held rung ended\n84\tat a brief nobody was owed. Replaced by a DERIVED `{aftermath}` slot\n85\t(`adjudicate_brief._aftermath`), which reads the declared gate authority for the\n86\ttiers actually shown and tells the session whether the re-attestation is its own\n87\tact or the owner's — rather than leaving it to read and interpret a dial\n88\tmid-verdict. `prompts/CATALOG.md` regenerated.\n89\t\n90\t**4. The doctrine says it once.** PROCESS.md §4 gains one clause in its fixed\n91\tpoints and links to PROCESS_OPTIONS.md \"Who performs the approval act\" (the\n92\truling, its two reasons, the division-of-labour table the owner asked for in\n93\t§2a, and the three holding mechanisms). OI-45 (b) gains the narrowing sentence.\n94\t`gate-advance` names the acceptor its procedure was already addressed to;\n95\t`spine-authoring` opens with \"authoring is not approving\"; `worker.template.md`\n96\tgains the NEVER clause and its close ritual now says \"minted or amended\". The\n97\ttwo reference surfaces follow the mechanism they describe:\n98\t`docs/enforcement-audit.md` gains the rule's row (with its three-fold honest\n99\tresidue), and `docs/registry-machinery-reference.md` records the other half of\n100\tthe amendment walk, plus the narrowing on the `Founded` row's OI-45 citation.\n101\t\n102\t**5. Tests**, all in the modules' existing style: five at the merge slot, four\n103\tat the reader, three at the trigger, and — counting the terminal-sequence\n104\tregression added at the round-3 rework below — six at the brief.\n105\t\n106\t**6. The two registry rows this lane's own code moved.** New code under an\n107\texisting row's Module leaves that row's cells stale, and the rule from the\n108\tWI-553 reading is that a lane re-points its own staleness IN-LANE rather than\n109\tdeferring it:\n110\t\n111\t- `LLR-158` (`Approved`) — its `code_symbol` named four symbols; the module now\n112\t  carries the shared two-tree walk and three more readers off it, and the row's\n113\t  `Detail` gained the sentence that explains them. Amended, **not flipped**:\n114\t  there is no status for a post-approval amendment, the row stays `Approved`,\n115\t  and the DRIFT against `docs/archive/last_approved/` is the signal.\n116\t- `IF-091` (`Drafted`) — `integrate` is now a second requestor of the seam, so\n117\t  it is named as one. Without it the new import is a cross-component crossing\n118\t  that no interface row declares.\n119\t\n120\tBoth warns were **introduced by this branch** and both are gone;\n121\t`check_trajectory` exits 0 with only the pre-existing `schedule -> trace`\n122\tcrossing standing. The amendment was checked against this row's OWN mechanism\n123\trather than assumed safe: `staged_approval_acts` returns `[]` and\n124\t`lane_approval_refusal` returns `None` over `4d0b972d..<staged tree>` — amending\n125\tis not approving — while `staged_spine_amendments` reports `LLR-158`, so the\n126\tamendment adjudication trigger (a) raises a row for it at merge. This lane's own\n127\tedit is the first customer of the arm the same lane shipped, which is the\n128\tintended shape and not a special case.\n129\t\n130\tRE-CHECKED AT THE FINAL TIP, because the reading above was taken at a staged\n131\ttree BEFORE the close, and the close changes an input this rung reads: draining\n132\t`docs/work/active/` empties `_claimed_specs`, so `_adjudication_lane` answers\n133\tFalse and this branch is judged as the work lane it is, with no exemption. Over\n134\t`4d0b972d..78e20e4e` the answers are unchanged — `staged_approval_acts` `[]`,\n135\t`lane_approval_refusal` `None`, `staged_spine_amendments` still reporting\n136\t`LLR-158`. The branch that added the refusal is merge-clean under it at the tree\n137\tthe integrator sees, which is the only tree the claim is worth anything at.\n138\t\n139\tThe amend-without-flip guard then fired on `LLR-158`, both arms, and both are\n140\tanswered rather than absorbed:\n141\t\n142\t- **The drift arm** — \"re-attest in this commit, or the change rides as SNAPSHOT\n143\t  DRIFT until the next sitting\". Riding as drift is not a miss here, it is the\n144\t  ruling: the lane may not re-attest its own amendment, and `intake.py snapshot`\n145\t  on this branch is precisely what deliverable 1 refuses. The drift IS the\n146\t  handover.\n147\t- **The `Hat-Refs` arm** — it says an unchanged cell cannot distinguish \"re-read\n148\t  and unchanged\" from \"never re-read\", so the answer belongs in the record.\n149\t  DELIBERATE: the cell stays inherited (`LLR-158` declares none and takes\n150\t  SR-178's `TEST-ENGINEER`). The amendment documents more of the SAME mechanism\n151\t  — one walk, four readers — and does not move the row's question, which is\n152\t  still how an amendment is DETECTED. Who may act on what is detected is a\n153\t  governance concern; it lives on the PROCESS surfaces and in `integrate`'s\n154\t  rung, not in this row's cells. Re-read, unchanged, and said so.\n155\t\n156\t### REVIEW-A rework: the dial filter the brief did not have\n157\t\n158\tRound 1 returned CHANGES-REQUESTED with one MAJOR finding, and it was right.\n159\t\n160\t**What was wrong.** `intake._released_drafted_rows` filters the minted\n161\tpopulation by the dial — a rung the owner still holds is not handed to an\n162\tadjudicator. `adjudicate_brief.first_approval_values` then RE-RESOLVES that\n163\tpopulation live at composition time, deliberately (a row minted at a merge is\n164\tclaimed later, and `red_tc_values`' rule says brief the world the judge is\n165\tactually in). But it re-resolved it from `trace.reattest_model`, which is\n166\tdial-blind by design, and never put the filter back. Re-computing live had\n167\tquietly become re-computing a WIDER question than the mint asked.\n168\t\n169\tThe consequence is not cosmetic. Under a mixed dial — `human_approval_through =\n170\t\"DevStg-Reqs\"` holds the SR tier and releases the LLR tier below it — the brief\n171\trendered a held `Drafted` SR beside a released `Drafted` LLR, marked BOTH\n172\t`[AWAITING FIRST APPROVAL]`, and derived a `--approves` argument naming both\n173\tregistries. That is a generated prompt instructing an adjudicator to perform a\n174\tsignature the owner owes, with the act's own recorded scope carrying it. At the\n175\tkit's shipped default dial (`DevStg-Release`, which holds every rung) every row\n176\tin the brief was the owner's.\n177\t\n178\t**Why the test suite did not catch it.** `_first_approval_repo` declared no\n179\t`docs/process.toml` at all, so its dial fell back to `DevStg-Release` — the\n180\tfixture was not this arm's scenario, it was the owner's, and it went green. The\n181\tfixture now declares `DevStg-Needs` explicitly, which is the honest statement of\n182\twhat the arm requires to exist.\n183\t\n184\t**The fix is a deletion, not a guard.** The rung table existed TWICE for one\n185\tcommit: `intake.APPROVAL_RUNG` keyed by registry (the mint) and\n186\t`adjudicate_brief._APPROVAL_RUNG_OF` keyed by tier (the amendment aftermath) —\n187\tand the first-approval brief, the third consumer, was wired to neither. Both\n188\tcopies are deleted. `agent_common.SPINE_APPROVAL_RUNGS` +\n189\t`human_approves_spine` is the one home, sitting beside the off-spine\n190\t`APPROVAL_RUNGS`/`human_approves` pair it mirrors arm for arm, unmapped-is-HELD\n191\tincluded. The mint, the amendment aftermath and the first-approval brief now all\n192\tread it; `intake.py` and `adjudicate_brief.py` both shrank. The predicate's\n193\tdocstring carries the reader-side contract the off-spine one states for writers:\n194\t*a filter applied only at the mint is a filter the brief does not have.*\n195\t\n196\tThree things follow from putting it in the derivation rather than the prose:\n197\t\n198\t- A held `Drafted` row is still SHOWN — it is part of the chain, and holding the\n199\t  chain is the owner's whole reason this act is the adjudicator's — but labelled\n200\t  `HELD FOR THE OWNER, NOT YOURS TO FLIP`, and it contributes no registry, so a\n201\t  session that ignored every word of the prose still cannot record it in scope.\n202\t- An SR whose chain holds no released `Drafted` row is dropped whole, and if\n203\t  nothing survives the assembler REFUSES, naming the dial. A brief whose every\n204\t  row is the owner's is not this arm's question.\n205\t- The template's opening claim (\"the rung they sit at is one the gate authority\n206\t  has released, so no human signature is pending behind you\") was TRUE of the\n207\t  mint and false of the brief. It now scopes itself to the `[AWAITING FIRST\n208\t  APPROVAL]` label and says which mechanism makes it true. `{registries}`' empty\n209\t  fallback string went with the refusal that made it unreachable.\n210\t\n211\t**Not taken, and why.** The finding's suggested remedy was to carry the minted\n212\trow identities as typed scope on the adjudication row and re-resolve only those.\n213\tThat is not available at the size the fix warrants: `intake._draft_row`\n214\tserializes only `wi_convert.COLUMNS`, and `parse_spec` drops any frontmatter key\n215\tthat is not a column — the trap `Supersedes`' own comment records — so a typed\n216\tscope carrier means a NEW REGISTRY COLUMN, which every adopting repo migrates\n217\tto. Against that, the module's stated rule (`red_tc_values`, restated in this\n218\tassembler's docstring) is that a brief re-derives its population rather than\n219\tremembering the mint's, and the adjudication lane is exclusive on trunk, so the\n220\tlive population at claim time is the authoritative one. What the finding's\n221\tfailure scenario actually turns on is the dial, and that is now filtered from\n222\tone home at both ends. The identity-scoping half is named here rather than done\n223\tsilently; if the owner wants it, it is a schema row of its own.\n224\t\n225\t### REVIEW-A rework: a withdrawal still owes re-approval\n226\t\n227\tRound 4 found the shared reader's last asymmetric edge: `Approved` → `Drafted`\n228\twas correctly absent from the lane refusal, but a status-only withdrawal was\n229\talso absent from `staged_drafted_rows`. That silently stranded the row even\n230\tthough the live re-attestation model correctly said it awaited `approve`.\n231\t\n232\tThe shared two-tree reader now classifies every transition into `Drafted` as an\n233\t`amended` Drafted row, with an empty content delta when Status alone moved.\n234\tThat record feeds the existing first-approval trigger; the approval-act reader\n235\tis unchanged and still refuses no de-approval. A reader regression pins both\n236\tsides of that split, and a trigger regression pins the resulting\n237\t`brief = \"first-approval\"` mint.\n238\t\n239\t### The harness, and the two reds it leaves\n240\t\n241\tFull unfiltered suite, re-measured after the status-only-withdrawal rework:\n242\t**3261 passed, 2 failed, 24 skipped in 576.13 s**. Both failures are the two\n243\treds analysed below, and neither is this row's. The smoke tier on the same tree:\n244\t1455 passed, 1 failed, 8 skipped in 21.15 s; the independent budget run measured\n245\t20.9 s against the 60 s ceiling — within.\n246\t\n247\tfig: cmd=\".venv/bin/python -m pytest -q -n auto\" rev=9cc58b2d\n248\tfig: cmd=\".venv/bin/python -m pytest -q -n auto -m smoke\" rev=9cc58b2d\n249\tfig: cmd=\".venv/bin/python scripts/check_smoke_budget.py --mode enforce\" rev=9cc58b2d\n250\t\n251\tThe first failure is\n252\t`tests/test_derive_stage.py::test_this_repo_s_committed_stage_is_current`, and\n253\tit is the DERIVED-ARTIFACT SPLIT, not a defect in the change:\n254\t\n255\t- It passed at `7021e4e1` and fails at `a68cc52a` — i.e. it is the registry\n256\t  amendment above, verified by running that single test in a worktree at each\n257\t  commit, not inferred.\n258\t- `docs/stage` is derived over the SETTLED spine rows, so amending an\n259\t  `Approved` row moves its input digest. Regenerating it in a scratch worktree\n260\t  at `a68cc52a` changes **only** the `fingerprint` and the `# computed … as-of`\n261\t  stamp: `stage`, `stage-ord`, `settled-stage`, `live-stage`, `per-phase`,\n262\t  `per-phase-live` and `drafted` all come back byte-identical. No rung moved.\n263\t  Re-confirmed at the final tip by `derive_stage.py --print`: every derived\n264\t  value above, `phase = 5` included, matches the committed `docs/stage` line for\n265\t  line, and `fingerprint` is the single field that differs.\n266\t- Regenerating it is not this lane's act. `docs/stage` is a declared generated\n267\t  artifact, and every commit that has ever written it is a trunk-side\n268\t  bookkeeping commit (`claim:`, `refresh:`, `log:`, `mint:`). The commit bar\n269\t  agrees and says so out loud: the `derived-stage --check` step SKIPS on a work\n270\t  branch with \"generated freshness is the trunk lane's\". The trunk lane\n271\t  regenerates after the merge and the red clears with it.\n272\t\n273\t**A second red appears only AFTER the close, and it is not this row's either.**\n274\tThe smoke tier run after the terminal move reds\n275\t`test_wi_convert.py::test_the_live_registry_round_trips_in_whichever_home_is_authoritative`\n276\ton `docs/work/cancelled/README.md: does not start with a +++ frontmatter fence`.\n277\tThe test takes one of two branches: with a claim in flight the converter must\n278\trefuse BY NAME (`drained-stop`, `active/`) and that refusal IS the test's claim;\n279\twith `active/` empty it runs the real folder round-trip. Closing WI-572 drained\n280\tthe last claim on this branch and moved it onto the second branch, where the\n281\tfolder walker does not skip the `README.md` files that sit in the work\n282\tsubfolders.\n283\t\n284\tNot introduced here, and checked rather than argued: the identical failure\n285\treproduces at `9eaeac93` — the trunk commit BEFORE WI-572 was claimed, whose\n286\t`docs/work/active/` does not exist at all. It is a latent defect masked by any\n287\tin-flight claim, so trunk meets it whenever the queue drains. Named here because\n288\tthis close is what unmasks it on this branch, and the next session should know\n289\tit inherited the red rather than caused it. Like the `docs/stage` red, it is a\n290\tharness-bar repair on its own row, not a fix to smuggle into this one.\n291\t\n292\t**A finding this row sharpened but does not fix.** That test asserts a\n293\ttrunk-side invariant with no work-branch exemption, while its commit-bar twin\n294\thas one. Until now the mismatch was nearly unreachable, because a lane amending\n295\ta settled spine row was rare. This row makes lane-side amendment the NORMAL\n296\tpath — a lane amends, an adjudicator approves — so the same red will now greet\n297\troutine lanes. Teaching the test the exemption its `--check` twin already has is\n298\ta real follow-on; it is a change to the harness's own bar and belongs on its own\n299\trow, not smuggled into this one.\n300\t\n301\t### D
